CM=ref('ELN: Management of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia, 2025','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12310532/')
q('Marked neutrophilic leukocytosis with a full myeloid maturation spectrum, basophilia and splenomegaly raises suspicion for which defining molecular lesion?', 'BCR::ABL1',[
 ('PML::RARA','That defines classical APL.'),('JAK2 V617F as the defining CML lesion','This is associated with Philadelphia-negative MPNs rather than defining CML.'),('MYD88 L265P','This is associated with selected lymphoid disorders.')],
 'The blood pattern suggests CML but requires confirmation', '**BCR::ABL1 encodes a constitutively active tyrosine kinase.**',['The classic Philadelphia chromosome is t(9;22).','Molecular and cytogenetic evaluation also establish the transcript and baseline disease features.'], '**Assess phase and treatment readiness.**',['Blasts, basophils and additional abnormalities matter.','Choose a TKI after reviewing comorbidity and goals.'],[['Pattern','Myeloid expansion and basophilia'],['Defining lesion','BCR::ABL1'],['Treatment class','Tyrosine kinase inhibition']],[CM],style='clinical_scenario')
q('What is the principal disease-directed mechanism of imatinib in CML?', 'Inhibition of BCR::ABL1 tyrosine kinase activity',[
 ('Inhibition of C1s complement','That is a different therapeutic pathway.'),('Direct blockade of PML::RARA differentiation arrest','That is the APL pathway.'),('Selective destruction of mature neutrophils without affecting the driver','Imatinib targets the oncogenic signaling mechanism.')],
 'CML illustrates driver-directed therapy', '**ATP-site TKIs suppress BCR::ABL1 signaling.**',['Several TKIs are available with distinct efficacy and toxicity profiles.','A normal CBC does not mean the molecular clone is eradicated.'], '**Monitor the molecular response and treatment tolerance.**',['Adherence and interactions influence exposure.','Long-term management includes quality of life and treatment goals.'],[['Driver','BCR::ABL1'],['Drug action','Kinase inhibition'],['Follow-up','Molecular response and toxicity']],[CM])
q('What distinguishes asciminib’s mechanism from conventional ATP-binding-site BCR::ABL1 inhibitors?', 'It binds the ABL myristoyl pocket',[
 ('It is a CD20 antibody','It is a small-molecule kinase-pathway drug.'),('It inhibits telomerase as its main action','That describes imetelstat.'),('It activates JAK2 as its therapeutic target','That is not the asciminib mechanism.')],
 'Allosteric targeting provides a different way to inhibit the same oncogenic protein', '**Asciminib is described as a STAMP inhibitor.**',['Its binding site differs from the ATP pocket.','The approved setting and dose depend on the current label and mutation context.'], '**Select treatment using evidence and patient risk.**',['First-line availability now exists in some countries.','Different binding does not eliminate resistance, interactions or safety monitoring.'],[['Conventional TKI','ATP-site targeting'],['Asciminib','Myristoyl-pocket targeting'],['Decision','Setting, mutations and patient factors']],[CM])
q('Which test is central to serial CML response monitoring during TKI treatment?', 'Quantitative BCR::ABL1 transcript measurement reported on the International Scale when applicable',[
 ('Serum ferritin alone','It does not measure the leukemia transcript burden.'),('A normal WBC count as a complete substitute','Hematological response is less sensitive than molecular monitoring.'),('Repeated D-dimer as the leukemia marker','This is not the CML response assay.')],
 'Molecular monitoring detects response depth beyond blood-count normalization', '**Use a validated assay and know the patient’s transcript type.**',['The International Scale supports standardized interpretation for suitable transcripts.','Atypical transcripts may require a different monitoring approach.'], '**Interpret the trend and milestones together.**',['Sample quality and assay sensitivity matter.','A surprising result can require confirmation and an exposure/adherence review.'],[['CBC','Hematological response'],['qPCR','Molecular burden'],['Scale','Standardization when applicable']],[CM])
q('What BCR::ABL1 International Scale level defines major molecular response in CML?', '0.1% or lower',[
 ('10% or lower','That is a much shallower response and is relevant to an early milestone.'),('1% or lower','This is not the MMR threshold.'),('Any detectable transcript regardless of amount','Response categories depend on the quantified level.')],
 'MMR corresponds to a standardized molecular depth', '**BCR::ABL1 at most 0.1% IS defines MMR.**',['Deeper responses include MR4 and MR4.5 with assay requirements.','Undetectable results must be reported with sample sensitivity information.'], '**Use response depth to support the appropriate clinical goal.**',['Survival control and treatment-free remission are related but distinct goals.','Do not stop therapy solely because MMR has been reached once.'],[['MMR','≤0.1% IS'],['Deeper response','MR4 / MR4.5'],['Stopping consideration','Requires sustained deeper response and eligibility']],[CM])
q('At 3 months of TKI treatment, BCR::ABL1 is 14% IS in a clinically stable CML patient. Under ELN 2025, what is the best next principle?', 'Review adherence, exposure and the response trajectory and confirm the result within the recommended interval before a context-specific switch decision',[
 ('A single value mandates an identical emergency switch for every patient','Confirmation and individual context matter.'),('Ignore the result until five years','This exceeds the favorable milestone and warrants review.'),('Stop all treatment because the transcript is measurable','Measurable disease does not justify abandoning treatment.')],
 'Milestones identify risk but are interpreted with trajectory and patient factors', '**The favorable three-month milestone is at most 10% IS.**',['Above 10% is a warning; confirmation within one to three months informs the unfavorable category.','Adherence, dose reductions and interactions can explain a delayed response.'], '**Choose the next step according to the full picture.**',['Mutation testing may be appropriate when resistance is suspected.','Do not let a single number replace clinical reasoning.'],[['3-month favorable','≤10% IS'],['Above threshold','Review and confirm'],['Decision','Trajectory, exposure and resistance assessment']],[CM],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A CML patient loses molecular response while taking a TKI. Which assessment is essential before labeling biological resistance?', 'Adherence, dosing and drug interactions, alongside appropriate confirmation and mutation assessment',[
 ('Assume every missed dose is irrelevant','Inadequate exposure can cause loss of control.'),('Use the original diagnosis alone to choose the next drug','The current mechanism and mutation profile may matter.'),('Ignore intolerance-related dose reductions','They can alter effective exposure.')],
 'Treatment failure can arise from the drug, its delivery or the disease', '**Confirm the pattern and review actual exposure.**',['A kinase-domain mutation can indicate a resistant clone.','Not every mutation has the same sensitivity profile.'], '**Select the next therapy deliberately.**',['Comorbidity can make an otherwise active drug unsuitable.','Advanced phase or multi-TKI resistance may prompt transplant planning.'],[['Delivery','Adherence, dose and interactions'],['Biology','Mutation and phase assessment'],['Choice','Active and tolerable next treatment']],[CM],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which BCR::ABL1 mutation confers resistance to imatinib and standard second-generation ATP-site TKIs, requiring a specifically active strategy?', 'T315I',[
 ('JAK2 V617F','This is a different gene and MPN pathway.'),('NPM1 mutation','This is an AML-associated alteration, not the classic CML gatekeeper mutation.'),('HFE C282Y','This is an iron-regulation variant.')],
 'A gatekeeper mutation can change TKI selection', '**T315I requires drugs with demonstrated activity in that context.**',['Ponatinib and appropriately selected asciminib strategies are relevant options under current guidance.','Vascular and other safety risks affect choice and dose.'], '**Do not treat every kinase-domain mutation as interchangeable.**',['Use a validated mutation assay and expert interpretation.','Consider disease phase and prior resistance history.'],[['Mutation','T315I'],['Implication','Resistance to several standard TKIs'],['Choice','Specifically active strategy with risk assessment']],[CM])
q('A CML patient develops recurrent pleural effusions while receiving dasatinib. Which interpretation is most appropriate?', 'A recognized TKI toxicity that may require interruption, dose adjustment or a change in treatment after evaluation',[
 ('Pleural effusion is impossible from dasatinib','It is a recognized adverse effect.'),('Every effusion proves blast-phase transformation','Other drug and disease causes must be assessed.'),('Continue unchanged without checking respiratory status','Symptomatic toxicity requires review.')],
 'Long-term TKI success includes tolerability', '**Dasatinib can cause pleural effusion and has an important pulmonary vascular safety profile.**',['Evaluate competing cardiac, infectious and malignant causes.','Severity and recurrence guide the management choice.'], '**Protect disease control while addressing toxicity.**',['Avoid unsupervised permanent discontinuation.','Choose an alternative with the patient’s cardiopulmonary and other risks in mind.'],[['Drug clue','Dasatinib'],['Problem','Pleural and pulmonary toxicity'],['Response','Assess cause and modify treatment appropriately']],[CM],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which condition is central to considering treatment-free remission in chronic-phase CML?', 'Sustained deep molecular response with suitable treatment history and access to frequent high-quality molecular monitoring',[
 ('A single normal CBC after one month','This is insufficient.'),('One MMR result followed by no further testing','Stopping requires a deeper sustained response and follow-up.'),('Stopping automatically after exactly one year for every patient','Eligibility is individualized and evidence-based.')],
 'Treatment-free remission is a monitored strategy, not casual nonadherence', '**Select patients using current eligibility criteria.**',['Duration of TKI exposure and deep response matter.','The patient must understand the monitoring commitment.'], '**Plan early detection and treatment restart if molecular relapse occurs.**',['Loss of MMR is a key restart trigger in standard pathways.','Even successful treatment-free remission requires ongoing follow-up.'],[['Eligibility','Sustained deep response and appropriate history'],['Infrastructure','Frequent reliable qPCR'],['Safety','Defined restart plan']],[CM])

